WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
I'm in Cape May, at the southern tip of New
Jersey, where the US Army Corps of Engineers

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.500
have just finished rebuilding this beach.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.249
A dredge is going offshore, and it's like
a vacuum,

00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:14.339
and it's sucking a sand-and-water mix up into
the dredge.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:15.950
Then it travels back toward the shore

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.980
and pumps that sand-and-water mix onto the
beach.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.770
And then bulldozers and construction equipment
will shape that beach

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:25.849
and build that template, that's designed to
reduce the damages from storms.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:28.779
We're using multiple hopper dredges and miles
of pipe,

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:32.460
we're using construction equipment, bulldozers,
and excavators,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.030
all to complete this job.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.530
We are demobilising from the site,

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.460
so all this equipment is going to another
section in Cape May,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.879
and we'll begin dredging and beachfill operations
at that location.

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:46.100
Miami Beach in America, the Gold Coast in
Australia, the beaches of Dubai:

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.820
around the world, engineers dredge sand from
offshore and pump it back onto beaches.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.329
Not only because the tourists demand it,

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:58.940
but more importantly because those beaches
protect the folks living next door from the

00:00:58.940 --> 00:00:59.969
ocean.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:04.000
And as sea levels rise, beach erosion is happening
faster and faster.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.600
Well, in New Jersey, there's lots of infrastructure
that's at risk from storms.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.040
Boardwalks, utilities, roads, homes, businesses.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.210
And so this process is the best means we have
to reduce storm damages

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.020
in a cost-effective and an environmentally
acceptable way.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.980
But there are other uses for sand, too:

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.880
every concrete building in the world, every
skyscraper,

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.140
is basically just a load of sand, like this,
stuck together with cement.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.711
And the world is running out of it.

00:01:30.711 --> 00:01:35.070
I know, that sounds strange, but the construction
industry uses billions of tons of sand every

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.070
year.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.150
So the obvious answer would seem to be: use
sand from elsewhere, right?

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.610
We've got whole deserts and ocean floors full
of the stuff.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.869
But those sands aren't useful.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:49.890
Desert and sea sands are too fine, too smooth,
they don't stick together properly,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.380
either for making concrete or for rebuilding
a beach like this.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.340
It has to be this kind of sand:

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.649
sand that's been created by moving water and
waves over aeons.

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:04.540
This is not a renewable resource, not on any
human timescale.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.510
And if it's not there to protect the coast:

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.680
then the folks who live nearby should start
getting worried.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.630
This project was first completed in 1990,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.750
and we've come back numerous times over the
years

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.750
to renourish and pump additional sand onto
the beach.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.190
If we didn't pump this sand into this area
and built an engineered system,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.160
the communities would be at higher risk of
damages

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.420
from hurricanes, nor'easters, and other storm
events.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.140
Now, running out of sand doesn't mean that
we're suddenly going to be unable to build

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.140
anything.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.010
It just means that as supply goes down, the
price of construction will go up...

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.620
and there are going to be some rough market
forces.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.940
No-one's considering stripmining the Jersey
shore any time soon,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.040
but journalist Vince Beiser spent years investigating

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.760
illegal sand mining in the developing world,
and brought back reports of

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:55.440
disappearing islands, devastated ecosystems
and murderous black-market sand mafias.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.630
Hundreds of people have been killed over sand.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:03.100
Now, here, the Corps of Engineers are keeping
a community safe,

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.890
some birds safe, and making sure there'll
still be a beach here for tourists this summer.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.040
But in a hundred years, who knows?

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.220
Maybe New Jersey will get a better offer for
this sand.

